  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Personal care refers to non-medical, hands-on assistance with daily activities—such as bathing, grooming, dressing, and mobility—designed to help individuals maintain hygiene, dignity, and independence at home. These services are essential for seniors or those with disabilities, and may include, among other tasks, meal preparation and medication reminders. Key Aspects of Personal Care Services Hygiene & Grooming: Bathing, showering, hair care, oral hygiene, and nail care. Dressing & Mobility: Assistance with clothing and transferring between beds or chairs. Toileting & Continence Care: Discreet support with bathroom needs. Daily Living Assistance: Light housekeeping, meal preparation, and medication reminders. Support & Independence: Aimed at helping people remain in their homes, avoiding institutions. Types of Personal Care ProductsBeyond services, personal care involves products used for cleaning, health, and beauty, which are categorized by function: Cleansing: Soaps, body washes, and facial cleansers. Hair Care: Shampoos, conditioners, and styling products. Skin & Sun Care: Moisturizers, lotions, and sunscreen. Oral Care: Toothpaste and mouthwash. Personal Grooming: Deodorants, hair removal, and feminine care products. Types of Personal Care Support In-Home Care Providers: Professional caregivers offering tailored support, often arranged via home care agencies. Personal Care Assistance (PCA): Often funded via Medicaid or private pay, providing dedicated support to individuals with mobility issues or chronic conditions. These services are often provided by licensed agencies and are aimed at ensuring safety while promoting independence.",
